  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule, and the rule is enabled. Its first run succeeded with no alerts. I haven't opened the dashboard in a browser, so I don't know yet if all its panels display data.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** Each document in `kibana_sample_data_logs` is one web request, timestamped by `timestamp`. The main fields are:\n- `response` / `response.keyword`: the HTTP status, stored as a string.\n- `bytes`: response size.\n- `clientip`: the visitor's IP address.\n- `url`, `request`, `extension`, `agent`, `machine.os`, `referer`.\n- `geo.src` / `geo.dest` / `geo.coordinates`: location.\n- `hour_of_day`: a computed field.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-3f118194 Web logs overview\"** (id `f4b67e0d-5ecf-4343-a4a1-aa3692fc08e5`) has 8 panels and shows the last 7 days by default:\n- **Top row (single numbers):** total requests, unique visitors (distinct `clientip`), average bytes per request, total bytes served.\n- **Requests over time:** a line chart.\n- **5xx errors over time:** a bar chart.\n- **Requests by response code:** a bar chart.\n- **Top 10 requested URLs:** a horizontal bar chart.\n\nKibana accepted and saved all 8 panels. The four charts use ES|QL queries that Kibana doesn't check when it saves, so those are the panels to look at when you first open the dashboard.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-3f118194 5xx spike\"** (id `1f594930-c225-40a0-9d53-a0def8bddbd0`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`:\n- It runs every minute.\n- It counts requests whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" over the last 5 minutes.\n- It fires when that count is more than 10.\n\nIt has no actions attached, so when it fires it records an alert in Kibana but sends no notifications.",
